#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069802} :activity/id #uuid "f87c9121-8769-5847-8c38-b9b5235f669f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048167 :topic/id #uuid "84e6e80d-04d2-5f31-acfa-65879d5c3d8e" :topic/math-academy-id 444} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069802 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070030 :tutorial/content "Consider the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x^2+2x+5}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral doesn't resemble any of the standard integrals we have encountered so far.\n\nHowever, notice that we can complete the square in the denominator as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+5 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+({1}^{2}-{1}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{1}^{2})-{1}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of ${\\color{red}{4}}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4[(x+1)^{2}/4+1]}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}/4+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x+1)^{2}/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+1)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this now resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arctan u + C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x+1}{2}\\right).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x+1}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x+1)/2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!" :tutorial/id #uuid "fa12f0db-7152-56d9-b243-3e761f5f2786" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "902d72dc-3d73-5330-9c00-fadbefe5a559" :step/math-academy-id 442844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068853 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068507 :question/id #uuid "4b6820ef-32a8-5d26-84f6-a464db90129a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14577"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348359}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "cb1059e4-1e66-5fbd-b127-18f2fa4cf7eb" :step/math-academy-id 442845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068504 :question/id #uuid "648e9e50-03bf-56ba-a4ad-caf142c9628a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14574"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319264} {:db